<p>HubSpot is one of the most recognised names in business software. It has been around for nearly two decades, it powers thousands of companies, and it has built a reputation as a serious growth platform. If you have ever searched for a CRM, marketing tool, or sales pipeline, you have seen HubSpot's name come up.</p>

<p>But here is the question most small business owners eventually ask: is HubSpot built for me, or is it built for companies ten times my size?</p>

<p>That is a fair question. And comparing DUSA to HubSpot is a useful exercise, because the two platforms take very different approaches to the same problem. Both want to help you grow. They just disagree on how much complexity that should involve.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>What HubSpot does well</h2>

<p>Credit where it is due. HubSpot is a powerful platform. Its CRM is well built, its reporting is detailed, and its ecosystem of integrations is enormous. For mid-size companies with dedicated marketing teams, HubSpot offers depth that is hard to match. But if you are a small business still figuring out what you need from a CRM, our <a href="/blog/crm-small-business">CRM guide for small business</a> is a good starting point.</p>

<p>The content management system is robust. The sales pipeline tools are polished. The academy and certification programmes have created a whole industry of HubSpot-trained marketers. If you have a team of ten people who all need to collaborate on complex marketing campaigns, HubSpot gives you the tools to do that.</p>

<p>None of that is in dispute.</p>

<h2>Where HubSpot gets complicated for small business</h2>

<p>The challenge starts with pricing. HubSpot's free tier is useful for getting started, but it is limited in ways that matter. You get basic CRM functionality, some email marketing, and a handful of forms. The moment you need more than the basics, you are looking at paid tiers that escalate quickly.</p>

<p>The Starter plan begins at around $20 USD per month. That sounds reasonable. But most small businesses outgrow it fast. The Professional tier jumps to $890 USD per month for Marketing Hub alone. Add Sales Hub Professional and you are well past $1,000 USD per month. These are not small numbers for a sole operator or a team of two.</p>

<p>Then there is the complexity. HubSpot has separate "Hubs" for marketing, sales, service, content, and operations. Each has its own feature set, its own pricing, and its own learning curve. Figuring out which Hub you need, which tier of that Hub, and which features are included at each tier is a project in itself.</p>

<blockquote><p>The most expensive software is the kind you pay for but never fully use because it was built for a team three times your size.</p></blockquote>

<h2>Where DUSA takes a different approach</h2>

<p>DUSA was built specifically for sole operators and small businesses. Not adapted for them. Not scaled down for them. Built for them from the ground up.</p>

<p>That means a few things in practice. First, everything is included in one plan. CRM, <a href="/blog/email-marketing-small-business">email marketing</a>, SMS, funnel builder, appointment booking, review management, automation, and pipeline management. There is no "which Hub do I need?" conversation. You get the full platform.</p>

<p>Second, the interface is designed for people who do not have a marketing coordinator to figure things out. The learning curve is measured in days, not months. You can be sending automated follow-ups and managing your pipeline within your first week.</p>

<p>Third, the pricing is flat and predictable. You know what you are paying each month, and you are not watching a usage meter tick upwards every time you send an email or add a contact.</p>

